feat(#67): deliver agent settings via the image, not the branch

The only delivery mechanism for agent config was the bind-mounted
worktree, which is why guardrails silently skipped runs whose branch
predated them (see #64). Bake config into the sandbox image instead:
the head branch strategy only ever bind-mounts /home/agent/workspace
(see run-sandcastle.ts's mount comments), so anything COPYed to
~/.claude/CLAUDE.md and ~/.claude/settings.json at image-build time
survives that mount and reaches the agent regardless of which branch
is checked out. Landing it at Claude Code's user-level config paths
means it merges with, rather than replaces, this repo's own
project-level CLAUDE.md / .claude/settings.json — no extra merge logic
needed. Both baked files are content-empty (an explanatory-only
CLAUDE.md, an empty settings.json) since this ticket is only the
channel; ticket 02 already asserts the loud failure this replaces, and
tickets 13-15 decide what flows through the channel.

Also has run-sandcastle.ts set two env markers on the docker sandbox —
SANDCASTLE_SANDBOXED and SANDCASTLE_ROLE (implement/review) — per the
issue's request. Nothing reads them yet; they're the prerequisite gate
for anything that must not apply to interactive host sessions. No
project-level config copy was added in this ticket, so there's nothing
to gate on the sandbox marker yet — host-interactive sessions never
call buildDockerSandbox at all, so they're unaffected by construction,
and the post-mortem skill's read of .sandcastle/logs/*.log is untouched
by this diff.

Note: this change could not be verified with an actual `docker build` +
container run — this sandbox has no docker binary/socket (verified: it
IS a sandcastle container itself), which is a structural limit of
reviewing sandcastle's own Dockerfile from inside a nested sandcastle
run, not something specific to this diff. The COPY ordering was checked
against install.sh's actual contents (it only writes to
~/.claude/downloads) to rule out the installer clobbering the baked
config.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
EOF
